    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Athletes and coaches are constantly looking for innovative ways to expedite and enhance strength and volume training responses. Previous research demonstrated a clear correlation between increased core temperature and fatigue onset during strength and volume training. This muscle fatigue can be rapidly reversed by extraction of heat from the body core. A novel device that puts the hand in a mild vacuum and cools the palmar surface, rapidly extracts considerable heat from the core. Palm cooling applied between sets of physical exercises has shown to be effective in increasing the performance of male athletes. Since female subjects have not been specifically tested, this study investigated strength conditioning in women and whether the palm-cooling technique could enhance their performance to a similar extent as in men although both sexes have different body compositions and hormonal environments. The study involved strength exercise sessions three times a week for a period of six weeks. Every session consisted of five sets of pushups whereby per set each subject executed pushups until exhaustion. Three weeks were dedicated to baseline testing in which nine sessions were completed with three minutes of only rest without cooling in between sets and the other three weeks comprised of nine sessions with 3 minutes rest with cooling in between sets. The results confirm that palm cooling had similar positive effects on strength and endurance development of female athletes. Heat extraction through the palm could thus be a novel and effective way to improve athletic performance in females.",
